er section\n184\t  binds, and the section it was shown in can be recorded in `block_id`. If\n185\t  the host verifies against the shown section only, a quote from elsewhere\n186\t  cannot bind. Which document a host names is the host's choice, and the\n187\t  pages recommend the whole source version.\n188\t- The packaged reading example is neither case: it verifies the attempted\n189\t  sections of one pass joined with newlines, or `conflict.txt` for the\n190\t  reported conflict, and writes no anchor sidecar (H9) `(r4)`. Its README says so and says it is not a model for anchoring `(r3)`.\n191\t  r2 said the example verifies \"against the shown section only\", which was\n192\t  wrong.\n193\t- `docs/literature/index.md:11` (\"GND keeps each claim tied to its\n194\t  passage\") is rewritten to cite the anchor rule `(r3)`.\n195\t- A test pins both sides with one source and one quote from its second\n196\t  section: bound when the sidecar and the document are the whole source;\n197\t  refused with `anchor:binding-mismatch: source span or excerpt differs`\n198\t  when the sidecar's `document_sha256` names the first section and the\n199\t  document is that section, so the span rule refuses and not the digest\n200\t  check `(r3)`. The same test reads the pages for the rule's one statement\n201\t  and the citations.\n202\t- The reading example's code and fixture do not change (R0.4).\n203\t\n204\t### LR3 — a labelled reference set and one recorded run\n205\t\n206\t- A labelled set the example reads: either the packaged regression corpus\n207\t  and its answer key (H24), with an overlay beside it where the reading\n208\t  stages need sections and no change to its bytes, or a second fixture\n209\t  beside `reading_fixture/` (D1) `(r3, r4)`.\n210\t- One live run over it through the producer protocol of LH1, recorded and\n211\t  replayed as LH2 specifies (D3). The record gives recall and precision per\n212\t  stage (broad, targeted) under the scoring rule of D4, and names the\n213\t  producer, the budget and the roster.\n214\t- The literature page shows the figures beside the scoring rule, the set's\n215\t  size and the sentence of R0.2, with #226's own figures (H14) as the row a\n216\t  host compares against.\n217\t\n218\t## 3. Dependencies and artifact homes\n219\t\n220\t| Phase | Needs | Homes it changes |\n221\t|---|---|---|\n222\t| LR1 | D7 and D8 ruled, or the plan gate accepting their proposals | the home D8 names (proposed `docs/guides/documents.md`), `src/gnd/skills/gnd-extract/references/extraction-contract.md` (one citing line, absolute URL, H25), `docs/literature/commands.md` (one citing line), a new `tests/test_lr1_verify_failure_listing.py`, `docs/release-notes.md` |\n223\t| LR2 | D2 ruled | `docs/reference/anchors.md`, `docs/literature/index.md`, `docs/literature/commands.md`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/README.md`, a new `tests/test_lr2_quote_scope.py`, `docs/release-notes.md`, the interim draft in `notes/issue-reply.md` `(r4)` |\n224\t| LR3 | LH1 and LH2 of literature-host-interfaces implemented and merged; D1, D3, D4 and D9 ruled | a new fixture folder under `src/gnd/examples/literature/` or the regression corpus (D1), `reading.py` only if LH1 leaves a scoring hook or a settings field to add (D9) `(r3)`, the literature pages, a new `tests/test_lr3_reference_set.py`, the packaged-example inventories the guards name, `notes/issue-reply.md` |\n225\t\n226\tOrder: LR1 and LR2 in either order, LR3 last. The graph is acyclic. LR1 is\n227\tthe first ready phase: it needs no other milestone. LR3 is an outline until\n228\tthe code of LH1 and LH2 is on `main` and its rulings exist.\n229\t\n230\tOther writers. literature-host-interfaces changes\n231\t`src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py` and the literature pages. One\n232\twriter at a time on `reading.py`: LR1 and LR2 do not touch it, and LR3\n233\tstarts after LH2 merges. LR1 and LR2 change `docs/literature/commands.md`,\n234\tand LR2 `index.md`, by a few lines `(r3)`; the seco