{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_nbSXwK0Ade86eYNfqc92Huhe","content":"60\tmodel, document, projection, and population binding.\n61\t\n62\tGiven those completed artifacts, the contextual verification command is:\n63\t\n64\t```sh\n65\tgnd verify model.orm.yaml extracted.population.json --artifacts artifacts --format json\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\tVerification validates the handoff before checking the population. Missing,\n69\tmalformed, or stale sidecars produce input refusal at band `3`. Read the\n70\t`extraction` block and the semantic verdicts independently. If your population\n71\twas authored directly and never extracted, explicitly use `--no-extraction`;\n72\tthe report records that the extraction path was skipped.\n73\t\n74\t## Reading a failed verification\n75\t\n76\tExit `1` means at least one alethic constraint or derivation failed. To see\n77\twhich ones, and on which rows, run the same command with `--format json`;\n78\tthe report's `outcome` is then `violated`. A model that includes another\n79\tfile needs that file at the path its `includes` entry names, next to the\n80\tmodel.\n81\t\n82\t1. The failing constraints are the entries of `verdicts` whose `verdict` is\n83\t   `fail` and whose `modality` is `alethic`.\n84\t2. Each one gives the constraint's address in `constraint_ref`:\n85\t   `fact_types[<fact type>].constraints[<n>]` for a constraint on a fact\n86\t   type, `constraints[<n>]` for one at the top level. The address is in the\n87\t   model `gnd verify` composed. For a model without includes that is the\n88\t   file you wrote. For a model with includes, the root's constraints and the\n89\t   included ones are composed into one list, so the position can differ from\n90\t   the file that declared the constraint. `gnd flatten MODEL --out DIR`\n91\t   writes a `gnd-flattening/1` record whose `origins` map each composed\n92\t   address to the file that declared it (`origin`), its address there\n93\t   (`original_address`) and its `id` (`element_id`); a fact type's composed\n94\t   position is the origin whose `element_id` is the fact type\n95\t   ([the record's format](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md)).\n96\t   `constraint_type` names the kind, `message` says what the constraint\n97\t   requires and what broke it, and `offending_facts` lists the witnesses of\n98\t   the violation. A witness's shape depends on the kind of constraint: the\n99\t   violating facts as objects of role values for the uniqueness failure\n100\t   below, for instance, or a bare instance for a mandatory constraint it\n101\t   does not meet. Read each witness with `message`, which says what it is.\n102\t3. The failing derivations are the entries of `derivations` whose `verdict`\n103\t   is `fail`. Each names itself in `derivation_ref` and lists what went\n104\t   wrong in `findings`. `derivations` is `null` for a model that declares\n105\t   none.\n106\t4. A `warning` verdict is a deontic violation. It is reported and does not\n107\t   change the exit.\n108\t5. A model can mark roles as sensitive. Then `message` holds a marker and\n109\t   `offending_facts` holds one marker row, in place of the values. Whether\n110\t   you can see the values (`--unredacted`, or `GND_DISCLOSURE=internal`) is\n111\t   set by the [disclosure mode](../reference/disclosure.md).\n112\t6. The text output (without `--format json`) prints the same constraints and\n113\t   messages, but not the rows.\n114\t\n115\tThe packaged literature reading example writes a population with two labels\n116\tfor one subject, which breaks a uniqueness constraint. These steps list it:\n117\t\n118\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->\n119\t```python\n120\timport json\n121\timport shutil\n122\timport subprocess\n123\timport sys\n124\tfrom importlib.resources import files\n125\t\n126\t\n127\tdef failed(report):\n128\t    \"\"\"Steps 1 and 3: the failing constraints, then the failing derivations.\"\"\"\n129\t    constraints = [v for v in report[\"verdicts\"] if v[\"verdict\"] == \"fail\" and v[\"modality\"] == \"alethic\"]\n130\t    derivations = [d for d in report[\"derivations\"] or [] if d[\"verdict\"] == \"fail\"]\n131\t    return constraints, derivations\n132\t\n133\t\n134\t# The literature reading example writes a population that breaks one rule.\n135\tsubprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-m\", \"gnd.examples.literature.reading\", \"run\", \"--out\", \"run\"],\n136\t               capture_output=True, check=True)\n137\t# The model includes a vocabulary file; both go in the same folder.\n138\texample = files(\"gnd\").joinpath(\"examples\", \"literature\")\n139\tfor part, name in ((\"reading_fixture\", \"reading.orm.json\"), (\"depth_fixture\", \"vocabulary.orm.json\")):\n140\t    shutil.copyfile(example.joinpath(part, name), f\"run/{name}\")\n141\t\n142\tdone = subprocess.run([\"gnd\", \"verify\", \"run/reading.orm.json\", \"run/reported-conflict.population.json\",\n143\t                       \"--no-extraction\", \"--format\", \"json\"], capture_output=True, text=True)\n144\treport = json.loads(done.stdout)\n145\tprint(\"exit:\", done.returncode, report[\"outcome\"])\n146\tconstraints, derivations = failed(report)\n147\tfor verdict in constraints:\n148\t    print(verdict[\"constraint_ref\"], verdict[\"constraint_type\"])\n149\t    for witness in verdict[\"offending_facts\"]:\n150\t        print(\"  \", witness)\n151\tfor derivation in derivations:\n152\t    print(derivation[\"derivation_ref\"])\n153\t    for finding in derivation[\"findings\"]:\n154\t        print(\"  \", finding)\n155\t```\n156\t\n157\t```text\n158\texit: 1 violated\n159\tfact_types[subject_label].constraints[0] uniqueness\n160\t   {'subject': 'alanine-in-vacuum', 'label': 'Alanine dipeptide observation'}\n161\t   {'subject': 'alanine-in-vacuum', 'label': 'Conflicting reported label'}\n162\t```\n163\t\n164\tThe flattening record of this model maps\n165\t`fact_types[subject_label].constraints[0]