{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_XS8RBpSm047Z1K7Jf24ukrwk","content":"1\t# Draft reply to #226 (all three requests)\n2\t\n3\tStatus: draft, first written in phase LR2 on 2026-11-23 and completed in\n4\tphase LR3 the same day. The owner posts it; nothing here is posted. Update\n5\tthe merge status before posting: on 2026-11-23 the three phases are in pull\n6\trequests 243 (LR1), 244 (LR2) and the LR3 pull request, none merged and none\n7\treleased.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tThanks for the detailed measurements. Two of your three requests have\n12\tanswers on `main` once the open pull requests for them merge; neither is in\n13\ta release yet.\n14\t\n15\t**3. Why `verify` reports a failure, by constraint and row.** The JSON\n16\treport already carries this. The documents guide now has a section,\n17\t\"Reading a failed verification\", with the steps and a runnable example: the\n18\tfailing constraints are the `verdicts` with `verdict: \"fail\"` and\n19\t`modality: \"alethic\"`, each with its `constraint_ref`, `constraint_type`,\n20\t`message` and `offending_facts`, and the failing derivations are the\n21\t`derivations` with `verdict: \"fail\"`. For a model with includes,\n22\t`constraint_ref` is an address in the composed model; `gnd flatten` writes a\n23\trecord that maps it back to the declaring file and the constraint's `id`.\n24\t`gnd verify` itself is unchanged.\n25\t\n26\t**2. Must a quote lie inside the section the model was shown?** No. An\n27\tanchor binds a span to the document whose digest the sidecar names, and the\n28\tspan may lie anywhere in it; GND has no notion of a section. If your host\n29\tnames the whole source version as the anchor document, a correct quote from\n30\tanother section binds, and you can record the shown section in `block_id`.\n31\tIf it names only the shown section, that quote is refused with\n32\t`anchor:binding-mismatch`. So the 44 claims you dropped for quoting outside\n33\tthe shown section were dropped by your host's rule, not GND's. The other 38,\n34\twhere the participant or predicate was not in the quote, are a host rule\n35\tGND's anchor check does not make. The anchor reference states the rule, and\n36\tan anchor still shows where a value occurs, not that the text supports the\n37\tclaim.\n38\t\n39\t**1. A reference recall and precision.** The literature example now ships\n40\ta reference set: the twelve development papers of the regression corpus\n41\twith 31 labelled facts, and one recorded run of the reading stages over it\n42\twith Claude Sonnet as the reading agent. After the broad pass it found 9 of\n43\t31 facts (precision 9 of 10); after the targeted pass, 27 of 31 (precision\n44\tof the targeted rows 18 of 23, two of the misses a right pair of names under\n45\tthe wrong predicate). The run cost USD 0.29 and replays with no model call;\n46\tthe record names the model, the CLI flags and the prompt.\n47\t\n48\tPlease read those figures as a measurement of one run on this set, not as\n49\twhat you should get. The papers are short paraphrases written to state their\n50\tclaims plainly, and the set scores a fact by the section it was read from,\n51\twhile you scored quotes, so the two are not directly comparable and the run\n52\tdoes not say where your gap comes from. What it gives you is a labelled set\n53\tyour own producer can run over, scored the same way\n54\t(`reference_set.score(your_run)`), next to this one."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_nQwGsd4RNa4E20mZCJVsGpcz","content":"1\t# Reading reference set\n2\t\n3\tA labelled set for the [multipass reading stages](../reading_fixture/README.md),\n4\tand one recorded run over it, so a host can compare its own recall and\n5\tprecision with a measured reference. It is an overlay on the\n6\t[regression corpus](../README.md): the corpus, its answer key and its\n7\texpected outcomes are not changed.\n8\t\n9\t**A figure here is a measurement of one run.** It names its producer, its\n10\tsettings and this set, and says nothing about other papers, other models or\n11\tother settings.\n12\t\n13\t## What is here\n14\t\n15\t| File | What it is |\n16\t|---|---|\n17\t| `<document>.txt` | The corpus's 12 development documents, byte for byte. They are synthetic paraphrases of public works, written for regression tests; no document is the text of its work |\n18\t| `corpus.orm.json` | The corpus's flat model, as JSON |\n19\t| `roster.json` | One source per document; one section per corpus passage, `<document>-p<n>`. Section `p1` runs from the start of the document (its title and identifier lines) to the end of the first passage and is read by the broad pass; every later passage is read by the targeted pass. Each later section has one question, so the targeted pass reads them all. No question is derived from the labels |\n20\t| `profile.json` | A caller root's profile; review, calibration, conflict and absence are off |\n21\t| `labels.json` | The gold facts of the answer key, each with the sections its roles are anchored in; 31 facts: 11 `work_introduces_method`, 8 `method_builds_on_method`, 12 `method_is_used_in_field`. `work_builds_on_method` is derived by the model, not read, and is left out |\n22\t| `run/` | The recorded run, a `literature-reading-run/2` folder |\n23\t| `run-record.json` | Who produced it and how: model, CLI version, flags, the prompt template and its SHA-256, the output schema, the spend and the `evidence_sha256` |\n24\t| `score.json` | The score of `run/` |\n25\t\n26\t`reference_set.build()` builds every file but the run, the record and the\n27\tscore from the packaged corpus; a test holds the committed files equal to\n28\tit.\n29\t\n30\t## The scoring rule\n31\t\n32\tThis section is the rule's one home.\n33\t\n34\t- A produced row is **found** when one gold fact of the same document has\n35\t  the same fact type and the same role values, and the section the row was\n36\t  read from is one of the sections that gold fact is anchored in. Values\n37\t  are compared after Unicode NFC, case folding and collapsing whitespace,\n38\t  and nothing else: a spelling the gold does not use is not found.\n39\t- A row is **wrong predicate** when a gold fact anchored in that section\n40\t  has the same values under another fact type.\n41\t- Each gold fact is found at most once. Every produced row counts in\n42\t  precision, including rows outside every gold fact's sections.\n43\t- **Precision** per pass is found rows over produced rows. **Recall after a\n44\t  pass** is the gold facts found so far over all gold facts.\n45\t\n46\tThe grain is the section: a produced fact has no span of its own, only its\n47\tsection's. Scores taken at a finer grain, a quote's span, are not directly\n48\tcomparable.\n49\t\n50\t## The recorded run\n51\t\n52\tProducer: Claude Sonnet, `claude-sonnet-5-5`, through the `claude` CLI with\n53\tthe owner's login, one call per section, with structured output, tools\n54\tdisabled and no session kept (`run-record.json` has the flags and the\n55\tprompt). Budget: every byte of every document (7,708 bytes); all 46 sections\n56\twere read, 12 broad and 34 targeted. The owner set a USD 50 ceiling; the run\n57\tspent USD 0.29.\n58\t\n59\t| Pass | Produced | Found | Wrong predicate | Precision | Recall after the pass |\n60\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n61\t| broad (opening sections) | 10 | 9 | 0 | 9 / 10 | 9 / 31 |\n62\t| targeted (later sections) | 23 | 18 | 2 | 18 / 23 | 27 / 31 |\n63\t\n64\tReplay re-derives the run with no producer call:\n65\t\n66\t```python\n67\tfrom gnd.examples.literature import reading, reference_set\n68\timport json\n69\t\n70\trecord = json.loads((reference_set.ROOT / \"run-record.json\").read_bytes())\n71\treading.replay(reference_set.ROOT / \"run\", fixture=reference_set.ROOT,\n72\t               expected_evidence_sha256=record[\"evidence_sha256\"])\n73\tprint(reference_set.score(reference_set.ROOT / \"run\"))\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\tThese documents are short paraphrases with one claim per passage, written\n77\tso that the claims are stated plainly. The run measures the stages on this\n78\tset only; it predicts nothing about longer or denser papers. To score your\n79\town run over this set, run the stages on this root with your producer and\n80\tcall `reference_set.score(your_run)`."}
